  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "What is the geometrical interpretation of the transformation represented by the matrix $A$ given below? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ given above represents a rotation of \n{{field-1}}\nradians clockwise about the origin.",
  "worked_solution": "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta >0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta <0$ we must have that $\\theta $ lies in $4$th quadrant. \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe principal value $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians *clockwise* about the origin.",
